      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Where are international teaching jobs advertised?",
            "answer": "<p>On job boards such as Tes, Schrole, Teach Away, SeekTeachers and LinkedIn, and through recruitment organisations such as Search Associates and ISS, which also run job fairs. COBIS lists jobs at British international schools through Tes.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I have to pay to find an international teaching job?",
            "answer": "<p>Mostly not. Teach Away and SeekTeachers are free for teachers. ISS and Schrole have free accounts with paid upgrades, and Search Associates charges a membership fee. Search Associates says any recruitment fee is paid by the school that hires you.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "When should I apply for an August start?",
            "answer": "<p>Early. Search Associates advises being registered a full year before you start, and openings peak from December to March. Jobs still come up all year.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need to go to a job fair?",
            "answer": "<p>No. Search Associates fairs are invitation-only for members, and ISS runs virtual and in-person events, but many teachers are hired through online interviews without attending one.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do I check a school is real?",
            "answer": "<p>Look it up in official directories: CIS, NEASC, MSA-CESS, Cognia, COBIS, the UK government's British Schools Overseas list, and the IB's Find an IB World School. Accreditation is voluntary, so not being listed doesn't prove a school is fake, but being listed is something you can check.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are the warning signs of a teaching job scam?",
            "answer": "<p>The UK's Disclosure and Barring Service lists unrealistic pay, a job offer without an interview, being asked for money, and suspicious emails or contact details. Never pay into a third party's account for a visa.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is it normal to pay visa fees myself?",
            "answer": "<p>The ILO's fair recruitment principles say workers shouldn't pay recruitment fees or related costs, including visas and background checks. In practice it varies, so get it in writing. U.S. J-1 sponsors do legally charge programme fees, so check the sponsor is on the State Department's list.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do I get a public-school teaching job in Canada?",
            "answer": "<p>Get certified by the province first, for example through the Ontario College of Teachers or New Brunswick's interim certificate. Then apply to school boards or districts, many of which post on Apply to Education.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
